WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
What the trained model shows, and what it doesn’t.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.088
For calibration, nanoGPT’s README reports a best validation loss of 1.

3
00:00:08.368 --> 00:00:23.536
4697 for its baby GPT config (6 layers, 6 heads, 384 channels, context 256) after about 3 minutes on one A100 GPU, and about 1.

4
00:00:23.816 --> 00:00:26.376
88 for a much smaller CPU config.

5
00:00:26.656 --> 00:00:33.291
The samples have the shape of a play, with speaker names, line breaks, and archaic-looking words, and almost no meaning.

6
00:00:33.571 --> 00:00:41.613
Those numbers are useful calibration for your project: if your run is far from them with a similar config, look for a bug before tuning.

7
00:00:41.893 --> 00:00:46.971
At 01:48:53 the lecture connects this to ChatGPT.

8
00:00:47.251 --> 00:00:50.813
What you built is the pretraining stage: a document completer.

9
00:00:51.093 --> 00:01:02.720
The same architecture at vastly larger scale, trained on vastly more data, followed by supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback, becomes an assistant.

10
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.589
That’s the arc from Studio 1’s first lesson, now with every piece of the first stage in your own code.

11
00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:20.731
A note on the repositories: as of the review date, nanoGPT’s README says it is old and deprecated and points to a newer project, nanochat, while leaving nanoGPT up.

12
00:01:21.011 --> 00:01:24.552
It remains a clear, readable reference for this lecture’s model.

13
00:01:24.832 --> 00:01:31.872
For your project, follow the lecture and treat nanoGPT as a reference to compare against, not a dependency.
